+ description: Step-by-step guide to add a new OpenClaw agent and connect it to a messaging channel (Telegram, Discord, Slack, Feishu, WhatsApp, Signal). Triggers on phrases like "create a new agent", "add a bot", "new team member bot".
+ A guide to adding a new agent to your OpenClaw setup and connecting it to a messaging channel.
+ ## When to Use
+ - Add a new AI agent to their OpenClaw gateway
+ - Connect a new bot to a messaging platform
+ - Set up an agent with its own workspace and identity
+ You will need:
+ - A name for the agent (e.g., "Luna")
+ - A messaging channel and its credentials
+ ## Step 1: Create the Agent Workspace
+ Create a dedicated workspace folder for the new agent:
+ /root/.openclaw/workspace-groups/{agentname}/
+ Inside, create these standard files:
+ **IDENTITY.md** — Who the agent is
+ # IDENTITY.md
+ - **Name:** {AgentName}
+ - **Role:** [e.g., Copywriter, Developer, Support]
+ **SOUL.md** — Agent personality and behavior
+ # SOUL.md
+ You are {AgentName}, an independent AI assistant.
+ Be genuinely helpful. Have opinions. Try before asking.
+ Keep private things private. Never send half-baked replies.
+ **AGENTS.md** — Startup instructions
+ ## On startup
+ 3. Read USER.md if present
+ Write important notes to memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md
+ **USER.md** — About the user
+ # USER.md
+ **HEARTBEAT.md**, **TOOLS.md** — Create empty files for these.
+ ## Step 2: Register the Agent
+ In `openclaw.json`, add the new agent under `agents.list`:
+   "id": "{agentname}-agent",
+   "workspace": "/root/.openclaw/workspace-groups/{agentname}"
+ ## Step 3: Connect a Channel
+ Add the channel account and a routing rule. Pick your channel:
+ ### Telegram
+ Get a Bot Token from @BotFather on Telegram.
+ Add account under `channels.telegram.accounts`:
+ "{agentname}": {
+ "{agentname}": {
+ "{agentname}": {
+ "{agentname}": {
+   "botToken": "paste-token-here",
+ ### Discord
+ Get a Bot Token from the [Discord Developer Portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications).
+ Enable **Message Content Intent** in bot settings, then invite the bot to your server.
+ Add account under `channels.discord.accounts`:
+   "token": "paste-token-here",
+ ### Slack
+ Create an app at [api.slack.com](https://api.slack.com/apps), enable Socket Mode, and copy both the App Token (`xapp-`) and Bot Token (`xoxb-`).
+ Add account under `channels.slack.accounts`:
+   "appToken": "xapp-...",
+   "botToken": "xoxb-..."
+ ### Feishu / Lark
+ Create an app at [open.feishu.cn](https://open.feishu.cn/app). Enable the bot, subscribe to `im.message.receive_v1`, and enable WebSocket mode.
+ Add account under `channels.feishu.accounts`:
+   "appId": "your-app-id",
+   "appSecret": "your-app-secret"
+ For Lark (global), also add `"domain": "lark"`.
+ ### WhatsApp / Signal
+ Link by scanning a QR code:
+ openclaw channels login --channel whatsapp --account {agentname}
+ openclaw channels login --channel signal --account {agentname}
+ Add an empty entry under the respective channel accounts:
+ "{agentname}": {}
+ ## Step 4: Add Routing Rule
+ In `openclaw.json`, add to `bindings`:
+   "agentId": "{agentname}-agent",
+     "channel": "{channel}",
+     "accountId": "{agentname}"
+ > Important: use `accountId`, not `account`.
+ ## Step 5: Apply and Verify
+ Restart the gateway to apply changes:
+ Verify:
+ ## Step 6: Pair (for DM channels)
+ For Telegram, Discord, WhatsApp, Signal — the user sends `/start` to the bot, then approves the pairing:
+ openclaw pairing approve {channel} {CODE}
+ ## Tips
+ - No new API keys needed — all agents share the existing model credentials.
+ - One channel is enough to bring an agent online.
+ - To add more channels later, just repeat Steps 3–4.